opencode updates itself in place: the background check asks the network
for the newest release on every start and, if it recognises how it was
installed, overwrites its own executable. For a packaged build that is
rpm's file, and the check contacts the network on every single start.

Setting "autoupdate": false in the system configuration directory does not
help. The check reads the global config, which loads only
~/.config/opencode/*.json; the /etc/opencode merge happens on a different
code path. Upstream's own OPENCODE_DISABLE_AUTOUPDATE is opt-out, so it
cannot be the distribution default either. The guard has to be here.

The explicit `opencode upgrade` command needs the same treatment, for a
second reason. With no terminal attached the command can block on a prompt
and never return, so `opencode upgrade` in a script or a CI job hangs
forever.

OPENCODE_ENABLE_AUTOUPDATE=1 restores upstream behaviour for both.

--- a/packages/cli/src/services/updater.ts
